  "summary": "**What I changed (frontend):** `src/pages/SigningPortalPage.jsx` — the whole post-OTP/signed UI was nested inside `session.isCurrentTurn`, but the backend only sets `isCurrentTurn` for `Sent`/`Viewed` signers. So the moment Anita signed and the session refetched, the portal flipped to \"Waiting on an earlier signer\" instead of confirming the signature. `Signed` is now a terminal status rendered outside the current-turn gate, with a regression test in `src/__tests__/SigningPortalPage.test.jsx`. Lint, build and tests pass.\n\n**Left for the backend repo (`life-cycle-backend`):** the signer email itself. `app/services/signers.py:352-356` sends only \"You have been asked to sign … Open your signing link and enter the one-time password sent to you\" — it never includes the session reference, the OTP or the expiry, unlike `app/services/lifecycle_negotiation.py:505-511` which does. Making the signing email match that shape (secure link reference = the signer/session id used in `/sign/<sessionId>`, the generated OTP, and `link_expires_at`) is backend work; the same applies to the next-signer and resend dispatches in `app/services/signing_sessions.py`. I did not fake it here.\n\nReading taken: the email-content part is the counterpart's, so here I fixed the one thing in this repo that breaks the sign→executed flow.",
